\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Definition}
\label{sec:method:problem}

Let $f_\theta$ denote a language model and $\mathcal{D}_0$ a corpus of human-written seed text.
We define a \emph{recursive generation chain} of depth $K$ as the sequence $\mathcal{D}_0, \mathcal{D}_1, \dots, \mathcal{D}_K$, where each $\mathcal{D}_k$ ($k \geq 1$) is produced by finetuning $f_\theta$ on $\mathcal{D}_{k-1}$ and sampling from the resulting model.
Depth $d{=}0$ denotes human-written text; depth $d{=}k$ ($k \geq 1$) denotes text generated by a model trained on depth-$(k{-}1)$ data.

Given a text passage $x$, the task is to estimate its recursive generation depth $d \in \{0, 1, \dots, K\}$.
This is an ordinal classification problem: depths carry a natural ordering, and $d{=}1$ is structurally closer to $d{=}0$ than $d{=}3$ is, since each generation step compounds distributional drift.

\subsection{Multi-Generational Benchmark Construction}
\label{sec:method:benchmark}

We construct a benchmark spanning three axes: model family, decoding strategy, and generation depth.

\paragraph{Model families.}
We select three 1--1.5B-parameter models with diverse pretraining corpora and architectures: Pythia-1.4B (EleutherAI), OLMo-1B (AI2), and GPT-2~XL (OpenAI, 1.5B).
Restricting to a narrow parameter range isolates the effect of architecture and training data from scale.

\paragraph{Decoding strategies.}
Each model is paired with three decoding strategies: nucleus sampling ($p{=}0.95$) \cite{holtzman2020_nucleus}, temperature sampling ($\tau{=}0.9$), and top-$k$ sampling ($k{=}50$).
These span the dominant modes of open-ended generation and induce qualitatively different entropy profiles.
Our findings on feature importance and discriminability are limited to this grid; transfer to untested models, scales, or strategies is not established.

\paragraph{Depth and scale.}
The primary benchmark covers depths d0--d3 across all $3 \times 3 = 9$ model--strategy cells, with 5{,}000 samples per depth per cell (180K samples total).
For depth extension experiments (\S\ref{sec:atlas}), we extend one pipeline (Pythia-1.4B, nucleus) to d0--d5.

\paragraph{Generation pipeline.}
Starting from a Wikipedia subset as seed text ($\mathcal{D}_0$), each generation cycle proceeds in three steps: (1)~LoRA finetuning \cite{hu2022lora} on $\mathcal{D}_{k-1}$, freezing base model weights and training low-rank update matrices ($<$1\% additional parameters; $r{=}16$, $\alpha{=}32$, lr $2{\times}10^{-4}$, batch size 8, 3 epochs, dropout $0.05$, architecture-specific targets); (2)~sampling 5{,}000 passages from the LoRA-finetuned model (max 512 tokens, BOS-seeded, no prompt conditioning) under the specified decoding strategy; (3)~the generated corpus becomes $\mathcal{D}_k$ for the next cycle.
Each depth is produced by a model explicitly trained on the prior depth's data, not by sampling from the unmodified base model.
Our BOS-seeded generation may amplify depth signal relative to prompted settings; a prompted-generation validation (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}) confirms signal persistence under topic-seeded conditions ($-$13.8\,pp vs.\ BOS-seeded).
There is no per-passage lineage across depths; cross-validation splits therefore do not introduce information leakage.

\paragraph{Reference model.}
All statistical features (\S\ref{sec:method:features}) are computed using Pythia-1.4B as fixed reference model, the smallest generator whose training corpus partially overlaps with the others.
For Pythia this constitutes self-model measurement, yet its accuracy (78.7\%) does not exceed GPT-2~XL (85.9\%); no systematic self-model advantage is observed.
Ablations with OLMo-1B and GPT-2 XL as alternative references yield 67--79\% accuracy (Appendix~\ref{app:ref_ablation}); reference model robustness is confirmed. Further discussion in Limitations.

\subsection{Statistical Feature Design}
\label{sec:method:features}

We extract a feature vector $\mathbf{f}(x)$ comprising 19 candidate features from the reference-model perplexity distribution over each passage $x$; ablation (\S\ref{sec:atlas:features}) shows the top 5 achieve 97.6\% of full-set performance, and variance inflation analysis reveals VIF${>}$10 for 14--16 features; effective independent dimensionality is ${\approx}$4--5.
The features fall into four categories (Table~\ref{tab:features}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{The 19 statistical features comprising $\mathbf{f}(x)$, grouped by category. All perplexity and surprisal features are computed from the reference model (Pythia-1.4B).}
\label{tab:features}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llr@{}}
\toprule
Category & Features & $n$ \\
\midrule
Perplexity & mean, var, skewness, kurtosis, & 9 \\
statistics & p10, p25, p50, p75, p90 & \\
\addlinespace
Surprisal & mean, var, entropy & 3 \\
statistics & & \\
\addlinespace
Lexical & type--token ratio, hapax ratio, & 4 \\
diversity & bigram entropy, trigram entropy & \\
\addlinespace
Repetition & repeated 2/3/4-gram fractions & 3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\emph{Perplexity statistics} (9 features) capture the per-token perplexity distribution via moments (mean, variance, skewness, kurtosis) and quantiles (p10--p90); tail quantiles prove especially informative as recursive finetuning erodes the high-perplexity tail (\S\ref{sec:atlas}).
\emph{Surprisal statistics} (3 features): mean, variance, and entropy of $-\log p(\text{token})$; surprisal variance captures second-order structure not redundant with perplexity moments.
\emph{Lexical diversity} (4 features): type--token ratio, hapax ratio, bigram/trigram entropy.
\emph{Repetition} (3 features): repeated $n$-gram fractions for $n \in \{2, 3, 4\}$.

\subsection{Effective Discriminable Depth}
\label{sec:method:edd}

Aggregate multi-class accuracy obscures whether discriminability degrades at specific depth boundaries.
We propose the \emph{Effective Discriminable Depth} (EDD) as an operational measure of how far into a recursive chain adjacent depths remain distinguishable.

\paragraph{Pairwise discriminability.}
Let $\mathcal{D} = \{0, 1, \dots, K\}$ denote the depth set and $T \in (0.5, 1.0]$ a discrimination threshold.
For each adjacent pair $(d, d{+}1)$, we define the pairwise discriminability $\mathrm{AUC}(d, d{+}1)$ as the area under the ROC curve of a dedicated binary Random Forest classifier (500 trees, $\lfloor\sqrt{19}\rfloor$ candidate features per split) trained on class-balanced data from $\mathbf{f}(x)$ (\S\ref{sec:method:features}) to distinguish depth-$d$ from depth-$(d{+}1)$ samples, evaluated via 5-fold stratified cross-validation.
Each pair uses an independent classifier, avoiding the decision boundary dilution that arises in multi-class settings (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).

\begin{definition}[Effective Discriminable Depth]
\label{def:edd}
The \emph{Effective Discriminable Depth} at threshold $T$ is:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{EDD}_T = \max \bigl\{ d^*{+}1 \,:\, \forall\, d' \leq d^*,\; \mathrm{AUC}(d', d'{+}1) \geq T \bigr\}
\label{eq:edd}
\end{equation}
where $d^* \in \{0, \dots, K{-}1\}$. If\, $\mathrm{AUC}(0,1) < T$, then $\mathrm{EDD}_T = 0$.
\end{definition}

$\mathrm{EDD}_T$ is the deepest depth $d$ such that every adjacent pair from $(0,1)$ through $(d{-}1, d)$ remains discriminable at level $T$.
The universal quantifier ensures EDD reports the longest \emph{contiguous} discriminable prefix rather than an isolated high-AUC pair at greater depth.\footnote{Our AUC profiles are empirically monotonically decreasing for the 1--1.5B base models (\S\ref{sec:atlas}), so in practice $\mathrm{EDD}_T = \max\{d'{+}1 : \mathrm{AUC}(d', d'{+}1) \geq T\}$. Qwen2.5-7B exhibits a U-shaped AUC pattern rather than monotonic decay; a base-model control shows non-monotonic decay persists without instruction tuning (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossscale}). For non-monotonic profiles, the contiguous formulation is more conservative (see Limitations).}
We set $T{=}0.75$ as default; sensitivity at $T \in \{0.65, 0.70, 0.75, 0.80\}$ is reported in \S\ref{sec:atlas}.

\paragraph{Classifiers.}
Our primary classifier is a Random Forest (200 trees, unconstrained depth, 5-fold stratified cross-validation).
We compare against CORAL ordinal regression \cite{coral2020}, DivEye \cite{diveye2026}, a 2-layer cross-entropy MLP, logistic regression, and a single-feature baseline using mean perplexity alone; results appear in \S\ref{sec:generalization}.
Full computational details (hardware, GPU hours, software versions) are in Appendix~\ref{app:compute}.

\section{The Depth Degradation Atlas}
\label{sec:atlas}

We present the Depth Degradation Atlas: a systematic empirical map of how statistical fingerprints evolve across recursive generation depths, models, and decoding strategies.
The atlas spans $3 \times 3 = 9$ model--strategy cells evaluated at depths $d \in \{0, 1, 2, 3\}$, with depth extensions to $d{=}5$ for cross-model EDD validation (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{In-Distribution Performance}
\label{sec:atlas:id}

Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} reports four-class Random Forest accuracy (5-fold CV, 200 trees) across all nine cells.
Accuracy ranges from 68.8\% (Pythia, top-$k$) to 85.9\% (GPT-2~XL, temperature), all exceeding the 25\% chance baseline by a wide margin.
Two axis effects emerge.
GPT-2~XL achieves the highest accuracy under every strategy (75.9--85.9\%), followed by OLMo (74.5--82.1\%) and Pythia (68.8--78.7\%).
Top-$k$ sampling yields the lowest accuracy in every model.
The mean within-model strategy gap is 9.2~pp, comparable to the 7.2~pp mean within-strategy model gap; decoding strategy and model family contribute roughly equally to performance variance.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Four-class RF accuracy (\%) with bootstrap 95\% CI (${\pm}$half-width; 10{,}000 resamples) across the $3 \times 3$ atlas; d1--d3 three-class accuracy in parentheses (chance = 25\% / 33\%). Depth-0 recall exceeds 98\% in all cells.}
\label{tab:main_matrix}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
& Nucleus & Temp.\ $\tau{=}0.9$ & Top-$k$ \\
\midrule
Pythia-1.4B  & 78.7{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(71.2) & 76.7{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(70.6) & 68.8{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(58.8) \\
OLMo-1B     & 78.9{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(75.0) & 82.1{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(76.3) & 74.5{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(66.1) \\
GPT-2~XL    & \textbf{84.1}{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(79.0) & \textbf{85.9}{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(82.6) & \textbf{75.9}{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(69.5) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Per-class recall distribution.}
Depth-0 recall exceeds 98\% in all 9 cells; human-written text is thus trivially separable from any generation depth.
Among generated depths, d2 consistently yields the lowest recall (46--73\% across cells), followed by d3 (58--82\%) and d1 (72--91\%).
For example, Pythia-1.4B nucleus achieves per-class recall of d0$\,{=}\,$99\%, d1$\,{=}\,$82\%, d2$\,{=}\,$59\%, d3$\,{=}\,$72\%.
The strongest d2 recall is obtained by GPT-2~XL nucleus (73\%).
The d2 trough reflects its position at the center of the depth gradient: d2 features overlap with both the d1 distribution above and d3 below and is therefore the most confusable class, consistent with the d2-vs-d3 pair as the EDD bottleneck (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).
Full per-class recall for all cells appears in Appendix~\ref{app:per_class}.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Feature Consistency Across the Grid}
\label{sec:atlas:features}

Despite the 17.1~pp accuracy spread across cells, a small feature family dominates in every setting.
Perplexity tail statistics as a feature group dominate depth discrimination: the 90th-percentile perplexity (\texttt{p90\_ppl}) ranks top-2 by Gini importance in all 9 cells; surprisal variance (\texttt{var\_surprisal}) in 8/9 under SHAP (7/9 under Gini; Appendix~\ref{app:permutation}).\footnote{Individual feature rankings should be interpreted cautiously given high multicollinearity (VIF${>}$10 for 14--16/19 features); the group-level dominance of perplexity tail statistics is robust across importance measures.}
Table~\ref{tab:feature_importance} shows the full per-cell ranking.

Cross-model feature-rank Spearman correlation averages $\bar{\rho} = 0.868$ (range $[0.619, 0.954]$): models agree on which features carry depth information even when they differ in absolute accuracy.
Among strategies, top-$k$ yields the highest cross-model rank correlation ($\rho = 0.811$): the deterministic vocabulary truncation imposes a more uniform feature landscape across architectures, at the cost of weaker overall signal (\S\ref{sec:atlas:strategy}).

Percentile-based features outperform their moment-based counterparts.
Token-level perplexity distributions are heavy-tailed: individual outlier passages can dominate the arithmetic mean of their depth class, whereas \texttt{p90\_ppl} is robust to such extremes while still capturing the tail erosion that signals generation depth.

Feature ablation confirms this concentration: the top-2 features (\texttt{p90\_ppl} + \texttt{var\_surprisal}) alone reach 91.1\% of full accuracy (71.5\%), and the top-5 features achieve 97.6\% (76.5\% vs.\ 78.4\%), with perplexity statistics alone reaching 76.6\% (vs.\ surprisal 73.2\%, other categories ${\leq}$47\%).
Variance inflation analysis reveals VIF${>}$10 for 14--16 of 19 features; effective independent dimensionality is ${\approx}$4--5.
Forward selection independently confirms the dominant pair: \texttt{p90\_ppl} is selected first in 6/9 cells, \texttt{var\_surprisal} in 3/9.

The feature hierarchy also shifts with depth.
At shallow boundaries (d1 vs.\ d2), \texttt{var\_surprisal} dominates importance; at deeper boundaries (d3 vs.\ d4, d4 vs.\ d5), tail perplexity statistics (\texttt{p90\_ppl}, \texttt{kurtosis\_ppl}) take over.
This transition aligns with model-collapse dynamics~\cite{shumailov2024_nature}: early recursion narrows the surprisal distribution (captured by its variance), while later steps erode the perplexity tail (captured by upper quantiles and kurtosis).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Top-5 features by Gini importance. \textbf{Bold}: top-2. \texttt{p90\_ppl} top-2 in 9/9; \texttt{var\_surprisal} 8/9 under SHAP (7/9 under Gini).}
\label{tab:feature_importance}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}l l@{}}
\toprule
Cell & Top-5 features (Gini importance, ranked) \\
\midrule
Pythia, nuc.       & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.17)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.15)}, m\_ppl\,(.11), var\_ppl\,(.08), p75\,(.07) \\
Pythia, $\tau$     & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.17)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.14)}, m\_ppl\,(.11), p75\,(.07), m\_surp\,(.07) \\
Pythia, top-$k$    & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.16)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.12)}, m\_ppl\,(.11), var\_ppl\,(.07), m\_surp\,(.06) \\
GPT-2\,XL, nuc.    & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.21)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.21)}, p75\,(.10), m\_surp\,(.07), m\_ppl\,(.06) \\
GPT-2\,XL, $\tau$  & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.25)}, \textbf{p75\,(.15)}, m\_surp\,(.13), var\_surp\,(.12), p50\,(.05) \\
GPT-2\,XL, top-$k$ & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.24)}, \textbf{p75\,(.12)}, var\_surp\,(.10), m\_surp\,(.09), ttr\,(.04) \\
OLMo, nuc.         & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.18)}, \textbf{var\_surp\,(.15)}, p75\,(.11), m\_surp\,(.07), ttr\,(.06) \\
OLMo, $\tau$       & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.21)}, \textbf{var\_surp\,(.16)}, p75\,(.12), m\_surp\,(.09), ttr\,(.05) \\
OLMo, top-$k$      & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.20)}, \textbf{var\_surp\,(.17)}, p75\,(.10), m\_surp\,(.07), m\_ppl\,(.05) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_atlas_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Atlas feature importance heatmap. Rows: 19 features grouped by category (\S\ref{sec:method:features}); columns: 9 model--strategy cells. Color intensity encodes RF Gini importance. The perplexity-tail and surprisal-variance blocks maintain high importance across all cells.}
\label{fig:atlas_heatmap}
\end{figure}

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Monotonicity and Inter-Depth Distances}
\label{sec:atlas:monotonicity}

Depth features exhibit strong monotonic trends: perplexity tail statistics (\texttt{p90\_ppl}, \texttt{mean\_surprisal}) decrease with depth (Spearman $\rho = -1.0$ across cell-level medians) while repetition features increase. This pattern reflects the complementary narrowing and self-repetition of distribution collapse.
Inter-depth distances are non-uniform: d0-vs-d1 AUC exceeds 0.99 in all 9 cells, whereas d2-vs-d3 ranges from 0.69 to 0.89; separating adjacent deep generations requires the tail statistics identified in \S\ref{sec:atlas:features}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_feature_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{Feature trajectories across depths $d{=}0$ to $d{=}3$, stratified by model (columns) and strategy (line style). Top row: \texttt{p90\_ppl} (monotonically decreasing). Middle: \texttt{var\_surprisal} (decreasing). Bottom: \texttt{rep\_3gram} (increasing). Shaded bands: interquartile range. The $d{=}0 \to d{=}1$ gap exceeds all subsequent inter-depth gaps.}
\label{fig:feature_trajectory}
\end{figure}

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{EDD Analysis}
\label{sec:atlas:edd}

We extend the benchmark to depth $d{=}5$ for all three models under nucleus sampling.
Each model's depth-3 LoRA checkpoint is used to finetune and generate depth-4 data, and the process repeats for depth~5; feature extraction uses the same reference model (Pythia-1.4B).
We train dedicated binary RF classifiers (500 trees) for each adjacent pair, as defined in Eq.~\ref{eq:edd}.

\paragraph{AUC decay profile.}
Figure~\ref{fig:auc_decay} plots the binary pairwise AUC decay curves.
For Pythia-1.4B (nucleus), AUC decays monotonically: d0-vs-d1 $= 0.999$, d1-vs-d2 $= 0.925$, d2-vs-d3 $= 0.823$, d3-vs-d4 $= 0.784$, d4-vs-d5 $= 0.683$.
The steepest drop occurs between the d1-vs-d2 and d2-vs-d3 pairs; the curve then flattens as the system approaches an indistinguishable regime gradually rather than crossing a sharp boundary.

\paragraph{AUC-excess contraction.}
The AUC-excess ($\mathrm{AUC} - 0.5$) contracts across successive depth pairs.
For the three-model mean profile (excess values $0.453, 0.362, 0.266, 0.183$ for pairs d1v2 through d4v5), the contraction ratio decreases from 0.80 to 0.69; convergence toward indistinguishability accelerates.
Per-model variation is larger (Pythia: 0.64--0.88) and reflects model-specific saturation rates.
This non-uniform contraction motivates the EDD metric (Eq.~\ref{eq:edd}): a depth threshold beyond which pairwise AUC falls below a practical detection limit.

\paragraph{Cross-model convergence at depth 4--5.}
The d3-vs-d4 AUC consistently clusters near the $T{=}0.75$ threshold across all three models (Pythia $0.784$, OLMo $0.765$, GPT-2~XL $0.750$), while d4-vs-d5 uniformly falls below $0.70$ ($0.681$--$0.684$).
The directional pattern is consistent, but statistical strength is stratified: bootstrap testing ($10{,}000$ resamples, $H_0{:}\, \mathrm{AUC} \leq 0.75$) yields $p = 0.001$ for Pythia (significant), $p = 0.091$ for OLMo (marginal), and $p = 0.505$ for GPT-2~XL (non-significant).
All three models cross the $T{=}0.75$ threshold within the d3--d4 region; a consistent depth-4 decay boundary emerges with graded confidence rather than uniform statistical significance.
The d4-vs-d5 saturation is unambiguous across models; this supports the interpretation of d3--d4 as a \emph{transition zone} from discriminable to indistinguishable regime.

\paragraph{EDD threshold sensitivity.}
Table~\ref{tab:edd_sensitivity} reports $\mathrm{EDD}_T$ across the primary 9-cell benchmark (depths 0--3) at four threshold values.
At $T{=}0.65$, all 9 cells achieve $\mathrm{EDD} = 3$; at $T{=}0.75$, 7 of 9 cells maintain $\mathrm{EDD} \geq 3$; at $T{=}0.80$, the count drops to 5 of 9.
The cells losing $\mathrm{EDD}{=}3$ first are consistently the top-$k$ configurations (Pythia top-$k$ at $T{=}0.70$, then OLMo top-$k$ at $T{=}0.75$), where the d2-vs-d3 pair is the consistent bottleneck across all 9 cells.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Number of atlas cells achieving $\mathrm{EDD} \geq 3$ at each threshold $T$, across 9 model--strategy cells (depths 0--3). The d2-vs-d3 boundary is the bottleneck; top-$k$ cells drop first.}
\label{tab:edd_sensitivity}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
& $T{=}0.65$ & $T{=}0.70$ & $T{=}0.75$ & $T{=}0.80$ \\
\midrule
Cells with $\mathrm{EDD} \geq 3$ & 9/9 & 8/9 & 7/9 & 5/9 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_auc_decay.pdf}
\caption{Binary pairwise AUC decay curves for three models (nucleus sampling, depths 0--5). Horizontal dashed lines mark $T \in \{0.65, 0.70, 0.75, 0.80\}$. All three curves converge below 0.70 at d4-vs-d5, while d3-vs-d4 AUC clusters in the range 0.750--0.784. Shaded bands: bootstrap 95\% CIs.}
\label{fig:auc_decay}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Decision boundary dilution.}
\label{sec:atlas:edd:dilution}
EDD uses binary-pair classifiers to avoid \emph{decision boundary dilution}: in Pythia nucleus, d2\,vs.\,d3 AUC degrades from 0.804 (4-class) to 0.689 (6-class), a 14.3\% relative decline despite identical data, because shared decision boundaries dilute per-pair capacity.
Binary classifiers eliminate this confound and isolate true adjacent-pair discriminability.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Strategy-Dependent Trajectories}
\label{sec:atlas:strategy}

The three decoding strategies induce qualitatively distinct degradation trajectories.
Nucleus ($p{=}0.95$) produces the most consistent accuracy (78.7--84.1\%) and serves as the reference trajectory.
Temperature ($\tau{=}0.9$) yields the highest accuracy in 2/3 models (GPT-2~XL 85.9\%, OLMo 82.1\%) because uniform logit scaling accelerates convergence to an attractor distribution~\cite{shumailov2024_nature}, amplifying the statistical fingerprint. However, this acceleration creates a boundary reversal risk: temperature depth-$N$ features approximate nucleus/top-$k$ depth-$(N{+}1)$ features, causing cross-strategy classifiers to systematically overpredict depth (\S\ref{sec:generalization}).
Top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) produces the lowest accuracy (68.8--75.9\%) because hard vocabulary truncation removes depth-discriminative low-probability tokens, though feature direction is preserved (cross-model $\rho = 0.811$).
The strategy effect (9.2~pp range) exceeds the model effect (7.2~pp); deployment-time knowledge of the decoding strategy therefore matters at least as much as the generator model.

\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether atlas accuracy (68.8--85.9\%) reflects genuine statistical regularities across eight generalization axes (five below, three in Appendix).

\subsection{Classifier Comparison and Surface-Text Ablation}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} evaluates bagging (RF) and gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM) on the same 19 features; per-cell spread ($\leq$0.4pp) establishes a feature-determined ceiling.
On Pythia nucleus, CORAL \cite{coral2020} (78.1\%) and DivEye \cite{diveye2026} (78.2\%) match RF within 0.6\,pp ($p>0.05$); all feature-based methods outperform CE-MLP (74.9\%) and logistic regression (75.0\%).
Binary detectors repurposed for ordinal classification (Binoculars + Fast-DetectGPT combined: 74.2\%) fall 4\,pp below RF (Appendix~\ref{app:detector_baselines}).

\paragraph{Surface-text ablation.}
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); zero-shot LLMs (DeepSeek V3.2, 36.2\%; Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, 30.4\%) also fail (43\,pp below RF; Appendix~\ref{app:zeroshot_prompt}); depth signal therefore resides in distributional statistics inaccessible from surface text.

\begin{table}[t]
